<ArticleLayout title="PostgreSQL CLUSTER and Clustered Indexes: When a Table Rewrite Helps" description="PostgreSQL has no clustered index. Test whether CLUSTER improves heap locality for range scans, then weigh ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locking, disk space and how fast the benefit decays." eyebrow="PostgreSQL physical design" published="2025-01-30" updated="2026-10-14" original>
  <Original />

  <ArticleOffer offer="investigation" title="Considering CLUSTER or pg_repack on a busy table?" text="CLUSTER takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock, pg_repack needs extra space, and the benefit fades as rows change. An investigation checks whether heap locality is really the bottleneck before you schedule a rewrite." />

  <h2 id="production-notes">Production notes (October 2026)</h2>
  <p class="production-intro">These notes add the diagnostic queries, decision criteria and validation steps the topic needs on a production system.</p>
  <h3>What CLUSTER changes</h3>
  <p><code>CLUSTER</code> rewrites a table so its rows follow an existing index's order. It can improve locality for queries that fetch many neighboring rows in that order. PostgreSQL does not maintain that physical ordering as later inserts and updates occur, so any gain can fade as the table changes.</p>
  <p>PostgreSQL has no clustered index in the SQL Server or MySQL InnoDB sense, where the table is stored in index order and kept that way. Every PostgreSQL table is a heap, and every index, including the primary key, is a separate structure. <code>CLUSTER</code> is the closest equivalent, but it is a one-time reorder, not a maintained property.</p>
  <p>This is different from partitioning, which splits rows into separate tables, and from adding an index, which leaves the table's physical order unchanged. A range scan that already uses a selective covering index may gain little from a rewrite.</p>

  <h3>Which query might benefit?</h3>
  <p>Look for a query that follows an index but fetches many heap rows scattered across pages. If a time-range scan reads a large number of heap blocks for relatively few useful rows, placing nearby index values near one another on disk may reduce that work. If the query is index-only, highly selective or already limited by CPU, rewriting the heap may not help.</p>
  <p>Use <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> on a safe read query and compare the same time window before and after on a production-like copy. Record heap blocks read, execution time and the index path; check warm and cold cache when I/O is the suspected bottleneck.</p>

  <h3>Measure before scheduling a rewrite</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify a high-cost range or ordered-read workload and capture its plan, buffers, latency and row count.</li>
    <li>Check whether physical correlation is actually poor for the relevant column. Compare the existing index strategy before attributing the issue to heap order.</li>
    <li>Test <code>CLUSTER</code> on a production-like copy, then repeat the same workload. Include table rewrite time, extra disk space and recovery impact in the decision.</li>
    <li>Recheck after normal writes. A one-time benchmark immediately after the rewrite can overstate long-term value.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>The operational cost is part of the result</h3>
  <p>The command is straightforward—<code>CLUSTER events USING events_occurred_at_idx;</code>—but it rewrites the table. PostgreSQL acquires an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock for the duration, blocking ordinary reads and writes. Its documentation calls for free disk space at least equal to the table plus its indexes. Test rewrite duration, replication or recovery impact and rollback on a copy before booking a maintenance window.</p>
  <p><code>CLUSTER</code> is a one-time operation: subsequent inserts and updates do not preserve index order. Measure again after a representative amount of normal writes. If the gain fades quickly, recurring rewrites may cost more availability than they return. Do not disable autovacuum as a supposed shortcut; vacuuming and clustering solve different problems.</p>
  <p>The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-cluster.html">PostgreSQL CLUSTER reference</a> documents the lock, disk and ordering behavior. If the question is whether a rewrite is worth the operational cost, bring the query and workload evidence to a <a href="/postgresql-performance-investigation">scoped investigation</a>.</p>
